WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:09.862
DG32-LITE is Deepgrid Semi's lockstep RISC-V motor-control chip, and this is its preliminary datasheet in twelve slides.

2
00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:16.554
Two cores run in lockstep inside a nine-by-nine millimetre, sixty-four-pin QFN.

3
00:00:17.104 --> 00:00:26.449
Everything here comes from the design as built for first silicon and from process nominals, not from measured parts, and each slide says which is which.

4
00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.938
Four facts set every design-in.

5
00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:33.568
The core runs from a one point eight volt rail.

6
00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:37.853
The input and output ring runs at three point three volts.

7
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:41.696
The whole chip runs from one fifty megahertz clock.

8
00:00:42.246 --> 00:00:50.851
And every signal comes out through forty-four pins of a sixty-four-pin package, with the other twenty pins given to supplies and ground.

9
00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.351
The datasheet lists nine features.

10
00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:58.822
Two RISC-V cores in lockstep with a fault comparator.

11
00:00:59.372 --> 00:01:04.756
Thirty-two kilobytes of SRAM and a sixty-four kilobyte boot ROM.

12
00:01:05.306 --> 00:01:13.980
Three-phase PWM with a hardware brake, four DShot channels, encoder and Hall inputs, a CORDIC and an on-die ADC.

13
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.607
Serial ports, SPI, I squared C and GPIO, with supervision.

14
00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:21.947
And it boots itself, with no management core.

15
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.442
Forty-four signal pins fall into eleven groups.

16
00:01:26.992 --> 00:01:33.240
Motor PWM and its sample trigger take seven, and so does the QSPI flash.

17
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.813
Position sensing takes six.

18
00:01:36.363 --> 00:01:40.384
Serial, SPI, JTAG and GPIO take fifteen between them.

19
00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:49.080
Three carry safety signals, including the fault output, and the analog inputs, I squared C, and clock and reset take two each.

20
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.335
The pins are placed by job.

21
00:01:52.885 --> 00:01:59.545
One side carries the motor: all six gate outputs, the sample trigger, the encoder and two Hall inputs.

22
00:02:00.095 --> 00:02:04.014
The opposite side carries the boot flash and the serial ports.

23
00:02:04.564 --> 00:02:13.648
The bottom holds JTAG, the clock and the resets, and the top holds I squared C, GPIO, the fault output and the supply-good inputs.

24
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:23.712
Every electrical limit in this datasheet is a design target or a process nominal, and first-silicon characterisation will replace it.

25
00:02:24.262 --> 00:02:33.490
The core supply runs from one point seven one to one point eight nine volts, and the input and output supply from three to three point six.

26
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:41.950
The analog inputs accept zero to one point eight volts, and absolute maximums sit just above the operating range.

27
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.404
Power is simple.

28
00:02:44.954 --> 00:02:50.315
All logic, memory and the ADC draw from the one point eight volt user rail.

29
00:02:50.865 --> 00:02:58.151
The other user rails are unused, and are tied to their nominal voltage only to keep protection structures biased.

30
00:02:58.701 --> 00:03:02.875
Bring up three point three volts before or together with one point eight.

31
00:03:03.425 --> 00:03:08.248
The tool estimate is about zero point four three watts at fifty megahertz.

32
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.627
Clocking is just as direct.

33
00:03:12.177 --> 00:03:21.417
One three point three volt clock input drives the whole chip at a fifty megahertz design target, and there is no on-die PLL.

34
00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:30.318
Two active-low resets are combined on the die with the power-on reset, so a board can use either one and pull the other high.

35
00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:36.500
The core itself reaches fifty-five to sixty-two megahertz after place-and-route.

36
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.339
The chip boots with no outside help.

37
00:03:40.889 --> 00:03:52.744
The ROM prints a banner on the console UART, reads and checks the flash image header, copies the image into SRAM over a quad-SPI read, and jumps to it.

38
00:03:53.294 --> 00:03:57.121
Code runs from SRAM, not in place from flash.

39
00:03:57.671 --> 00:04:04.003
If the flash is blank, a UART monitor starts instead, so a bare board can still be inspected.

40
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.625
These are the limits firmware can plan on.

41
00:04:08.175 --> 00:04:18.191
The UARTs run at one hundred fifteen thousand two hundred baud, SPI to twenty-five megahertz, and I squared C at one hundred or four hundred kilohertz.

42
00:04:18.741 --> 00:04:25.644
PWM dead-time reaches five point one microseconds, and the brake acts within two cycles.

43
00:04:26.194 --> 00:04:33.270
The ADC samples at about two hundred kilosamples per second, and on this silicon it runs untrimmed.

44
00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:41.747
The package is settled: a nine-by-nine millimetre QFN with a half-millimetre pitch and an exposed ground paddle.

45
00:04:42.297 --> 00:04:44.648
Three things are still preliminary.

46
00:04:45.198 --> 00:04:57.475
The pin map waits on the foundry's bond-diagram confirmation, the electrical section waits on characterisation, and the control core stays frozen for feature work until silicon test.

47
00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.451
So here is what is fixed and what is not.

48
00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:06.767
Fixed now: the package, the pin groups, the rails, the clock and the boot path.

49
00:05:07.317 --> 00:05:12.124
Measured at first silicon: every electrical limit, and the final pin map.

50
00:05:12.674 --> 00:05:19.700
The register-level detail lives in the engineering datasheet; this deck is the plan you build around.

